---
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const issueUrl = site.contact.url;
const title = `Corrections process | ${site.name}`;
const description =
  "How to report a factual error, broken source, or material omission to Everyday Tech Insight through the public GitHub issue tracker.";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/corrections/">
  <div class="page-shell">
    <Breadcrumbs
      items={[
        { name: "Home", href: "/" },
        { name: "Corrections", href: "/corrections/" },
      ]}
    />
    <article class="trust-content">
      <p class="eyebrow">Accuracy and updates</p>
      <h1>Corrections</h1>
      <p class="page-deck">
        Factual errors, broken primary sources, and material omissions should be
        reported so the affected guide can be reviewed.
      </p>
      <h2>Submit a report</h2>
      <p>
        Open a <a href={issueUrl}>public GitHub issue</a> with the article URL, the
        passage in question, the proposed correction, and a supporting source when
        available.
      </p>
      <p>
        No public correction email has been supplied or configured. The issue
        tracker is the current correction-reporting channel; an email address
        will not be invented for appearance.
      </p>
      <p class="notice">
        GitHub issues are public. Do not include private, confidential,
        financial, account, credential, or security-sensitive information.
      </p>
      <h2>Evaluation and response</h2>
      <p>
        A report should be checked against the cited and newly supplied
        evidence. The review considers whether the issue changes a factual
        claim, recommendation, limitation, source interpretation, or practical
        outcome. Submitting a report does not guarantee that a requested change
        will be accepted.
      </p>
      <h2>Dates and correction notes</h2>
      <p>
        Every article keeps its genuine first-publication date. The publication
        does not silently backdate an article or change dates merely to make
        content look older or newly refreshed. A modification or review date is
        added only when the corresponding substantive work actually occurred.
      </p>
      <p>
        Minor typographical, spelling, formatting, or link-label corrections may
        not receive a formal update note or modification date when they do not
        change meaning. A substantial factual correction should receive a
        transparent correction note on the affected article, together with an
        accurate modification or review date when applicable.
      </p>
      <p>
        The publication will not invent a correction history. If no formal note
        appears, that absence is not evidence that a guide has never received
        minor cleanup.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
